// coil.map — Straf3 course 1, "Coil"
//
// Valve 220 source. This file is the SINGLE source of the course geometry:
// straf3-map compiles it to both the collision hulls and the render mesh, so
// the thing you see and the thing you hit cannot drift apart. Edit it as a
// .map (TrenchBroom, Radiant) — do not mirror these numbers into code.
//
// No BSP and no PVS, so the world does not need to be sealed and there are no
// ceilings; brushes exist only where the player can reach them.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The line, and why each piece is the shape it is. Physics: gravity 800,
// jump 270, step 18, min_walk_normal 0.7. A jump lasts 0.675 s flat and peaks
// 45.6 units up. "ups" below is horizontal speed in units/second.
//
//   S0  y -832..32    Start room, 896 x 864, spawn in the west at angle 0.
//                     768 units of eastward run and a 90-degree left arc: a
//                     circle jump, executed inside the room, crossing the
//                     start line at the mouth already fast.
//
//   S1  y 32..1568    Strafe corridor, 448 wide, 1536 long. Pure acceleration.
//
//   S2  y 1568..2304  The ramp wave. ramp_a climbs 1:2 to a crest at z=112,
//                     ramp_b falls 1:2 back to z=0, ramp_c climbs at exactly
//                     45 degrees (surface normal z = 0.7071, seven thousandths
//                     above min_walk_normal — walkable, but only just), onto a
//                     flat launch pad at z=128.
//                     THE OPTIMISATION: the V between ramp_b and ramp_c is a
//                     trap for the slow. From the crest at (1792, z=112) it is
//                     352 units and +16 to the launch pad. Measured: 575 ups
//                     lands you flat on the pad with your speed intact, while
//                     below it you land on ramp_c's 45-degree face and the
//                     clip into that plane takes most of your speed away.
//
//   S3  y 2304..2816  The gully. 512 units from the launch lip (z=128) to the
//                     landing lip (z=64): a 64-unit drop buys 0.861 s of air.
//                     Measured: 600 ups clears it onto the flat approach pad,
//                     575 lands high on the climb-out. Falling in is not fatal —
//                     the near wall is a 3:2 slide (normal z = 0.5547, below
//                     min_walk_normal, so the sim reports Sliding: no friction,
//                     air rules, no jumping) down to a floor, and a 1:2 ramp
//                     climbs back out. It costs seconds, not the run.
//
//   S4  y 2816..3136  320 flat units. Long enough to straighten up, short
//                     enough that a botched gully exit is still felt here.
//
//   S5  y 3136..3680  The finish. 288 units of open air, no walls, onto a
//                     ledge 192 wide and 16 higher than the lip you left.
//                     Measured landing window 425..900 ups. Below 425 you land
//                     on ramp_bail_back instead and slide to the bottom of the
//                     pit, which walks you back up to the approach pad to try
//                     again — the ledge's front face is 272 units tall and
//                     cannot be climbed. The ledge is a quarter the width of
//                     the run-up you jumped from, so lateral drift ends the
//                     same way distance does.
//                     Above 900 you cross the finish line in flight and land
//                     in the pit past the ledge: the run still counts, which is
//                     why pit_floor reaches y=5376 rather than stopping at the
//                     ledge.
//
// The three surface families are coloured by what the simulation does with
// them, not by what they look like: straf3/ramp is walkable, straf3/ramp45 is
// walkable by 0.007, straf3/slide is not walkable at all.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// entity 0 — worldspawn
